# Architecture — {{productDisplayName}}

A modular monolith on the TrustOS Engineering Framework v{{frameworkVersion}},
generated from the `{{templateId}}` template.

## Principle

The framework owns the cross-cutting concerns; this repository owns the
product. Anything in the first list below has already been solved, tested and
reviewed once — reimplementing it here would create a second, weaker copy.

| Framework provides | This repository provides |
| --- | --- |
| configuration validation | product domain models |
| authentication and sessions | product endpoints |
| organization tenant isolation | product permissions |
| RBAC and route guards | admin screens |
| audit trail | product migrations |
| structured logging | product tests |
| error contract | |
| health and readiness probes | |

## Request lifecycle

```
  request
    │
    ├─ requestContextMiddleware   request id, log context, timing
    ├─ tenantScopeMiddleware      opens the per-request tenant scope
    │
    ├─ JwtAuthGuard               who is calling?   → request.actor
    ├─ TenantGuard                whose data?       → request.organizationId
    ├─ PermissionsGuard           may they do this? → deny by default
    │
    ├─ ZodValidationPipe          untrusted input → typed, stripped value
    ├─ handler                    product logic; queries carry the scope
    │
    ├─ AuditService.record        for every sensitive mutation
    └─ AllExceptionsFilter        any failure → the standard error body
```

The guard order is the security model. Authentication before tenancy before
authorization means an anonymous request to a tenant-scoped route returns 401
rather than 403, and a cross-tenant request never reaches a permission check.

The tenant scope is opened by **middleware**, not by the guard: a guard returns
before the handler runs, and the async context it entered does not reliably
survive that boundary. The middleware opens a holder for the whole request and
the guard fills it in.

## Tenant isolation

1. Every query against a tenant-owned model includes `organizationId`.
2. The organization comes from the access token, never from the request.
3. A lookup by primary key is verified with `assertTenantMatch` after loading.
4. A cross-tenant hit reports `not_found`, never `forbidden`.
5. Frontend filtering is never a control.
6. Every tenant-owned model has isolation tests.

## Adding a feature

1. Add the model to `prisma/schema/10-product.prisma` with `organizationId`,
   timestamps, `deletedAt` and an index on `organizationId`.
2. `npm run db:migrate -- --name add_your_model`
3. Add permission keys to `packages/product-domain/src/index.ts`.
4. Add the module under `apps/api/src/modules/`, following
   `modules/product`: a permission on every route, `@OrganizationId()` in the
   handler, audit on every mutation.
5. Add isolation tests beside the code.
6. Seed the new permissions onto roles in `prisma/seed.ts`.

## Dependency rules

- `packages/shared-types` has no runtime dependencies and is safe for the
  browser.
- `packages/product-domain` may depend on `shared-types` and on framework
  packages, never on an application.
- Applications may depend on any package. Nothing depends on an application.
- The admin app may only import `@trustos/shared-types`, `@trustos/errors` and
  `@trustos/validation` from the framework — the rest are server-only and would
  drag secrets or Node built-ins into a browser bundle.
